  "final_message": "Customers now have a `net_terms_days` setting (default 7), and finalized invoices set `due_at` from the customer's current term. The full suite passes: 33 tests, the 24 existing ones plus 9 new ones.\n\nChanges:\n- **`billing/models.py`**: `Customer` gains `net_terms_days: int`.\n- **`billing/customers.py`**:\n  - `create_customer(..., net_terms_days=7)` validates and stores the value.\n  - `update_customer(..., net_terms_days=...)` validates and updates it.\n  - Negative values, and non-integers including bools, raise `ValidationError`.\n- **`billing/invoices.py`**: `finalize` reads the customer's `net_terms_days` instead of the hardcoded `INVOICE_NET_DAYS`.\n- **`billing/api.py`**: `POST /customers` accepts `net_terms_days`, defaulting to 7. A negative value returns 400.\n- **`billing/serialization.py`**: `customer_json` includes `net_terms_days`.\n- **`tests/test_net_terms.py`**: new tests for the default, custom create, zero, negative rejection on create and update, update applying at finalization, and the API paths.\n- **`answer.json`**: written in the repo root with the one-sentence summary.\n\nChoices I made:\n- **Zero is allowed**, so a customer can be due on finalization. Only negatives are rejected, as you specified.\n- **`INVOICE_NET_DAYS` stays in `models.py`** as the default value rather than being removed.\n- **Existing customers** are not migrated. The store is in-memory with no persisted schema, so there is nothing to backfill.\n- **Changing terms affects only invoices finalized after the change.** Invoices already open keep their existing `due_at`.",
